{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":3,"query":"name=Stewart&limit=10&title=Politics On the Edge","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":[{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Politics on the edge ","subTitle":"the remarkable career of Paul MacEwan "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Stewart, Ian,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1953 July 2-"}],"role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author"}}],"nameIdentifier":"https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/entity/E39PCjyTFfVTpqJfkbGMxqWT6q"},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"biography"},{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"},{"@authority":"Medical Subject Headings","#text":"Biography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"rvmgf","#text":"Biographies."}],"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nsc"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"Nova Scotia"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2022"},"copyrightDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2022"},"issuance":"monographic"},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Halifax, NS :"}},"agent":{"namePart":"Nimbus Publishing,","role":{"roleTerm":"publisher"}},"dateIssued":[2022]}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"xii, 560 pages : illustrations ; 23 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"Paul MacEwan (1943-2017) was a singular politician, an outlier who successfully pushed the limits of Nova Scotia's political norms over the longest continuous service record in the history of the legislature. Yet he never achieved the sort of prominence such a record might command--though serving as Speaker (1993-1996), he was never made a cabinet minister. MacEwan's brand of constituent-centred politics saw him victorious in nine consecutive general elections over thirty-three years, a record unlikely to fall anytime soon. At the same time, he didn't fit into the club-like atmosphere of the legislature. Obsessive, at times bombastic, few politicians have been as polarizing as MacEwan. He made few friends and rarely socialized with other legislators; instead he was always \"on-call\" for his constituents. His electoral victories came under four different partisan labels--first as a New Democrat (1970, 1974, 1978), twice as an independent (1981, 1988) once as leader of his own short-lived Cape Breton Labour Party (1984), and finally as a Liberal (1993, 1998, 1999)--before retiring from politics in 2003. For a Canadian politician to change their electoral colours successfully even once is rare--MacEwan ran under four, a peripatetic record unparalleled in modern Canadian politics. Author Ian Stewart recounts Paul MacEwan's life, his austere home environment, his social convictions and electoral prowess, unorthodox style and, particularly, his singular dedication to constituents. From his longevity to his checkered partisanship to his obsessive personality, Paul MacEwan cut a remarkably distinctive profile. In all ways, MacEwan personified politics on the edge.\"-- Provided by publisher."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Ian Stewart."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references and index."},"Co-published by Cape Breton University Press.",{"@type":"additional physical form","#text":"Issued also in electronic format."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"n-cn-ns"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["MacEwan, Paul,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1943-2017"}]}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Politicians","geographic":"Nova Scotia","genre":"Biography"},{"@authority":"rvm","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["MacEwan, Paul,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1943-"}]}},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":"Politiciens","geographic":"Nouvelle-Écosse","genre":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Politicians"},{"@authority":"fast","geographic":"Nova Scotia"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"F1038.M12 S74 2022"},{"@edition":"23","@authority":"ddc","#text":"971.6/04092"},{"@authority":"lacc","#text":"cci1icc"}],"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Cape Bretoniana research series"}},{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Politics on the edge"},"originInfo":{"publisher":"Halifax, Nova Scotia : Nimbus Publishing, 2022"},"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"1774710498"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9781774710494"},{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)1289306722"}],"name":{"namePart":"Stewart, Ian, 1953 July 2-"}},{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Cape Bretoniana research series"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/99156602943803941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9781774710487"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"177471048X"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"2021388666"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"1289306545"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"F1038.M12 S74 2022"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":"Widener"},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/99156602943803941","processingDate":"2026-08-04T18:44Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"rda","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"NLC"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"211217"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20240530"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"99156602943803941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)","languageOfCataloging":{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"}}}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Politics on the edge ","subTitle":"a memoir from within "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Stewart, Rory","role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author"}}],"nameIdentifier":["https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/entity/E39PBJj4qrwxFvYyhXQrvVrpfq","https://isni.org/isni/000000007371614X."]},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"biography"},{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"},{"@authority":"aat","#text":"autobiographies (literary works)"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Autobiographies"},{"@authority":"rvmgf","#text":"Autobiographies."}],"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"enk"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"England"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2023"},"copyrightDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2023"},"issuance":"monographic"},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"London :"}},"agent":{"namePart":"Jonathan Cape,","role":{"roleTerm":"publisher"}},"dateIssued":"2023."}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"x, 454 pages ; 24 cm"},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"Over the course of a decade from 2010, Rory Stewart went from being a political outsider to standing for prime minister - before being sacked from a Conservative Party that he had come to barely recognise. Tackling ministerial briefs on flood response and prison violence, engaging with conflict and poverty abroad as a foreign minister, and Brexit as a Cabinet minister, Stewart learned first-hand how profoundly hollow our democracy and government had become.Cronyism, ignorance and sheer incompetence ran rampant. Around him, individual politicians laid the foundations for the political and economic chaos of today. Stewart emerged battered but with a profound affection for his constituency of Penrith and the Border, and a deep direct insight into the era of populism and global conflict.Uncompromising, candid and darkly humorous, Politics On the Edge is his story of the challenges, absurdities and realities of political life and a remarkable portrait of our age\"--Publisher's description."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Rory Stewart."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes index."},"Includes index."],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-uk---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Politicians","geographic":"Great Britain","genre":"Biography"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Cabinet officers","geographic":"Great Britain","genre":"Biography"},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":"Politiciens","geographic":"Grande-Bretagne","genre":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":"Ministres","geographic":"Grande-Bretagne","genre":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"eflch","topic":"History"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Cabinet officers"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Politicians"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Politics and government"},{"@authority":"thema","topic":"England"},{"@authority":"thema","topic":"EU (European Union)"},{"@authority":"thema","topic":"Autobiography: historical, political & military"